<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/the-islamic-golden-age">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Between roughly 750 and 1258 CE, the Islamic world was <strong>the global centre of science,
    medicine, philosophy and mathematics</strong> — a civilisation that translated the ancients,
    systematised them and pushed far past them. The House of Wisdom, the hospitals of Baghdad and
    Córdoba, and scholars from al-Khwarizmi to Ibn Sina carried the torch that Europe later
    picked up. Modern science has ancestors in many places; this was one of the decisive ones.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the golden age, in four achievements" widget="equationStep" config={goldenCfg} />

  <h2>The network behind the achievements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Paper technology, learned from China (751), made it scale:</strong> cheap books for a literate mercantile society — a printing-press effect three centuries before Europe's press.</li>
    <li><strong>Trade routes were knowledge routes:</strong> from Córdoba to Samarkand, merchants carried books alongside goods; pilgrims carried ideas between cities. Arabic became science's Latin.</li>
    <li><strong>Religion and science coexisted productively:</strong> astronomy served prayer times and qibla direction; inheritance law drove algebra; medicine served hospitals endowed as religious charity (waqf).</li>
    <li><strong>The decline is a historiography lesson:</strong> the Mongol sack of Baghdad (1258) matters, but the longer story is shifting trade routes, political fragmentation, and Europe's rise — "decline" narratives always need careful dating.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Gutas, Greek Thought, Arabic Culture; Lyons, The House of Wisdom; Saliba, Islamic Science and the Making of the European Renaissance.</p>
</TopicPage>
